> Module Uc

# Universal Credit: skeleton rules for 2026/27

This file holds a thin slice of Universal Credit for a single claimant: the
standard allowance, the housing costs element, the work allowance and the
earnings deduction, and the award that follows from them. It models only what
is needed to exercise the pipeline and the queries built on it.

Simplifications, stated so that nobody reads more into the rules than is there:

* Only single claimants are modelled.
* The work allowance is applied when the claimant has limited capability for
  work (`has_lcw`). Responsibility for a child is not modelled.
* The housing costs element is an input, taken as already worked out. Rent,
  the Local Housing Allowance cap, the under-occupancy deduction, housing cost
  contributions and the other steps of regulation 26 and Schedule 4 are not
  modelled. See `docs/model-gaps.md`.
* The LCWRA element, the amount for responsibility for children and young
  persons and every other element are left out. The maximum amount is the
  standard allowance plus the housing costs element.
* Unearned income is not modelled.
* `earned_income` is employed earnings as regulation 55 defines them, not
  gross pay: earnings after deducting income tax, primary Class 1 National
  Insurance contributions, relievable pension contributions and payroll
  giving
  ([regulation 55(5)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/55),
  checked 29 October 2026).

Catala's English syntax writes money with the `$` symbol. In this file the unit
is pounds sterling.

Every parameter below is defined only for assessment periods starting on or
after 6 April 2026. For an earlier date no definition applies, and Catala stops
with an error instead of returning a value from rates that do not apply.
Sources were checked on 29 October 2026.

## Scope declaration

```catala-metadata
declaration scope UniversalCredit:
  input assessment_period_start content date
  input earned_income content money
  input aged_25_or_over content boolean
  input has_lcw content boolean
  input housing_costs_element content money
  internal deduction_rate content decimal
  output standard_allowance content money
  output work_allowance content money
  output earnings_deduction content money
  output maximum_amount content money
  output award content money
```

## Standard allowance

Regulation 36 gives the amount of the standard allowance. The rates below were
substituted by [S.I. 2026/113](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/113),
with effect for assessment periods starting on or after 6 April 2026.

"single claimant aged under 25": £338.58, in the amount column
([regulation 36](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/36),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition standard_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06| and not aged_25_or_over
    consequence equals $338.58
```

"single claimant aged 25 or over": £424.90, in the amount column
([regulation 36](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/36),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition standard_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06| and aged_25_or_over
    consequence equals $424.90
```

## Housing costs element

The housing costs element is an input. It stands for the amount that
[regulation 26](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/26) and
Schedule 4 of the Universal Credit Regulations 2013 give for eligible rent,
taken as already worked out, per assessment period (checked 29 October 2026).
Nothing that sits between the rent and the element is modelled. The gap is
recorded in `docs/model-gaps.md`.

## Maximum amount

Section 8(2) of the
[Welfare Reform Act 2012](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/5/section/8)
makes the maximum amount the total of any amounts included for the standard
allowance, responsibility for children and young persons, housing costs, and
other particular needs or circumstances (checked 29 October 2026). Only the
first and third are modelled, and the third is an input.

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition maximum_amount equals standard_allowance + housing_costs_element
```

## Work allowance

Regulation 22(2) gives the amount of the work allowance. Both amounts were
substituted by [S.I. 2026/148](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/148)
in the version of the regulation dated 6 April 2026. The higher amount applies
when the award has no housing costs element, and the lower one when it has.
This file reads "contains an amount for the housing costs element" as an
element above zero, as PolicyEngine UK does. The reading is the project's, for
specialist review.

The three definitions below are parallel cases, not a rule with exceptions, so
their conditions exclude each other.

Single claimant who "has limited capability for work", where "the award
contains no amount for the housing costs element": £710.00, the higher work
allowance
([regulation 22(2)(a)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition work_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06|
      and has_lcw and not (housing_costs_element > $0.00)
    consequence equals $710.00
```

Single claimant who "has limited capability for work", where "the award does
contain an amount for the housing costs element": £427.00, the lower work
allowance
([regulation 22(2)(b)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition work_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06|
      and has_lcw and housing_costs_element > $0.00
    consequence equals $427.00
```

Regulation 22(1)(b)(i) covers a claimant for whom no work allowance is
specified. The whole of that claimant's earned income is then used, which this
file represents as a work allowance of zero, whether or not the award has a
housing costs element.

"in a case where no work allowance is specified in the table below"
([regulation 22](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition work_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06| and not has_lcw
    consequence equals $0.00
```

## Earnings deduction

The deduction rate is 55%, as regulation 22(1)(b) reads in the version dated
6 April 2026. The figure was substituted for 63% by regulation 2(1) of
[S.I. 2021/1283](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2021/1283), in force from
24 November 2021 (checked 29 October 2026).

"55% of the amount by which that earned income exceeds the work allowance"
([regulation 22](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition deduction_rate
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06|
    consequence equals 55%
```

The deduction is one money-times-decimal step, so Catala rounds it once, to the
nearest penny with halves rounded away from zero. That matches regulation 6(1).

"otherwise it is to be treated as a penny"
([regulation 6](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/6),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition earnings_deduction equals
    if earned_income > work_allowance
    then (earned_income - work_allowance) * deduction_rate
    else $0.00
```

## Award

Regulation 22(1) deducts earned and unearned income from the maximum amount
under [section 8(3) of the Welfare Reform Act 2012](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/5/section/8). The award cannot be negative.

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition award equals
    if maximum_amount - earnings_deduction > $0.00
    then maximum_amount - earnings_deduction
    else $0.00
```
